            "Critical care is a <strong>level of nursing</strong> before it is a place: level 0 is the ordinary ward; <strong>level 1</strong> is the patient at risk of deterioration, needing more frequent observations and a nurse who knows what she is looking for; <strong>level 2 (high dependency)</strong> is one nurse to two patients, with single-organ support — non-invasive ventilation, a vasopressor, close monitoring after major surgery or a severe eclampsia; <strong>level 3 (intensive care)</strong> is one nurse to one patient, with invasive ventilation or the support of two or more organs. Ghana has a few hundred intensive care beds for over thirty million people, concentrated in the teaching hospitals and the regional hospitals, so the question of <strong>who should occupy a bed</strong> is one the emergency nurse faces: the bed is for the patient who has a <strong>reversible</strong> critical illness and a reasonable prospect of recovering to a life they would value — the young woman with eclampsia and pulmonary oedema, the farmer with organophosphate poisoning who needs ventilating for three days, the trauma patient with a chest injury, the postoperative patient with sepsis — and not for the patient whose dying it will only prolong and make lonelier. That decision is made by senior clinicians with the family, but the nurse's observations of the trajectory are part of it, and the nurse who recognises that a patient is deteriorating on the ward early enough is the reason the bed is used for a rescue and not for a futile final day.",
