            "The nurse does not have to set a ventilator to nurse a ventilated patient safely, but must understand what it is doing. The ventilator delivers either a set <strong>volume</strong> or a set <strong>pressure</strong> with each breath; in a <strong>controlled</strong> mode it delivers every breath at a set rate, in an <strong>assisted</strong> mode (synchronised intermittent mandatory ventilation, pressure support) it supports breaths the patient triggers, and in <strong>continuous positive airway pressure</strong> the patient breathes alone against a background pressure. The settings on the screen are the <strong>FiO₂</strong> (fraction of inspired oxygen, 0.21–1.0), the <strong>tidal volume</strong> (about 6–8 mL/kg of ideal body weight — 400–500 mL for most adults; large volumes injure the lung), the <strong>rate</strong> (12–20), the <strong>PEEP</strong> (positive end-expiratory pressure, usually 5–10 cmH₂O, which holds the alveoli open at the end of expiration and improves oxygenation) and the <strong>inspiratory pressure or pressure support</strong>; the numbers the patient produces are the <strong>peak airway pressure</strong> (ideally under 30–35 cmH₂O), the <strong>exhaled tidal volume</strong> and the <strong>minute volume</strong>. The alarms are the nurse's business: a <strong>high-pressure alarm</strong> means something is obstructing the flow — the patient coughing or biting the tube, secretions, a kinked tube, bronchospasm, a pneumothorax, the patient fighting the ventilator; a <strong>low-pressure or low-volume alarm</strong> means a leak or a disconnection — the circuit has come apart, the cuff has deflated, the tube has come out; an <strong>apnoea alarm</strong> in a spontaneous mode means the patient has stopped breathing; a <strong>high-rate alarm</strong> means distress, pain, hypoxia or acidosis; and a <strong>low-saturation alarm</strong> means whatever is wrong is now reaching the blood. Alarms are <strong>never silenced without looking at the patient</strong>, and their limits are set for the individual patient at the start of the shift.",
