          paragraphs: [
            "A <strong>major incident</strong> is any event whose casualties, in number or type, exceed what the responding facility can manage with its normal resources — a bus crash on the Kintampo road with forty injured arriving at a district hospital with two doctors, a market fire, a stampede at a stadium, a building collapse, a gas-station explosion like the Atomic Junction disaster, a fuel-tanker fire, the June 2015 Accra flood-and-fire that killed over 150 people at the Kwame Nkrumah Circle, a cholera or meningitis outbreak in the north, or the floods that follow the spilling of the Akosombo dam. A <strong>disaster</strong> is a major incident that also disrupts the community's own ability to cope — homes, water, roads, the health facilities themselves. Ghana's response is coordinated by the <strong>National Disaster Management Organisation (NADMO)</strong> under the Ministry of the Interior, with regional and district offices, working with the Ghana National Fire Service, the National Ambulance Service, the Police, the Armed Forces, the Ghana Health Service, the Red Cross and the district assemblies; the health sector's own emergency preparedness and response is led by the Ghana Health Service's public health division and, at facility level, by the <strong>hospital major incident plan</strong>, which every nurse should have read before the day it is needed.",
            "The <strong>disaster management cycle</strong> has four phases that overlap: <strong>mitigation</strong> (reducing the hazard — building codes, drainage, fire safety, not building on waterways); <strong>preparedness</strong> (plans, training, drills, stockpiles, communication trees, agreements between facilities); <strong>response</strong> (the hours and days of rescue, triage, treatment and evacuation); and <strong>recovery</strong> (restoring services, rebuilding, the long tail of injury, disability, grief and mental illness). A facility's <strong>major incident plan</strong> sets out who declares the incident and how staff are called in ('stand by' then 'declared'); the <strong>incident command structure</strong> — an incident commander (usually the medical director or the senior clinician on duty), with chiefs for operations, planning, logistics and finance, and specific roles such as triage officer, treatment area leads, a nurse in charge of staffing, a communications officer and a family liaison officer, each with an <strong>action card</strong> that tells them what to do; the clearing of beds (the emergency unit emptied by discharging and moving every patient who can be moved, elective surgery stopped, theatre and the blood bank alerted); the <strong>triage area</strong> at the entrance and the <strong>colour-coded treatment areas</strong>; the supply of pre-packed major incident equipment, tags and documentation; security and the control of relatives, press and officials; and the stand-down and debriefing. The nurse's job on an ordinary day is to know where the plan and the action cards are kept, which role she would be given, and how she would be contacted; and on the day, to do the role on her card and nothing else.",
          ],
